## Reproduction

The reported flow asks for a Rust Hello World program, then follows up in Russian with a request to replace `Hello World` with `Bye world` in the previous assistant response. Before this PR, the follow-up did not edit the active assistant artifact and fell through to the generic fallback.

## Changes

- Route contextual `text_manipulation` turns through conversation history so follow-up replacements edit the prior assistant artifact, including generated code blocks.
- Keep replacement matching exact-first and punctuation-tolerant for multi-word matches such as `Hello World` versus `Hello, world`.
- Expand deterministic text/code operations across Rust and the browser worker: replacement, remove, append, prepend, trim/normalize whitespace, title/sentence/snake/kebab/camel/pascal case, URL/email/number extraction, word/line/character/occurrence counting, punctuation removal, sort lines/words, strip/join/reverse/number/comment/uncomment/indent/outdent lines.
- Move shared Rust edit helpers into `src/solver_handlers/text_edit_ops.rs` and mirror them under `tests/source`.
- Prevent semantic web search from intercepting text extraction requests just because the input text contains a live URL.
- Extend the shared EN/RU/HI/ZH operation vocabulary and regenerate the total-closure shards and browser worker embedded seed data.
- Raise the repository-local issue #408 benchmark profile to 28 researched sources x 20 deterministic local variations per source, with `minimum_pass_count = 560`.
- Sync the case study, raw benchmark research, requirements R293-R297, roadmap, vision, architecture notes, docs requirements, and changelog with the executable profile.

## Benchmark Scope

This PR now implements the requested repository-local 10%-style ratchet across all researched benchmark sources: 8 PR-referenced edit benchmark families plus 20 additional popular LLM benchmark sources, each with 20 deterministic local text/code edit variations. The local ratchet passes 560/560 cases.

This is still a repository-local pass-count ratchet, not an official upstream benchmark score. Publishing official full-upstream benchmark results would require imported or explicitly referenced benchmark payloads, provenance/license review, official-style runners and scoring, CI budget, and per-benchmark ratchets for those upstream records.

